WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.615
Rachel Kim: Alright, it’s 10:02, let’s get started. Hope everyone’s doing okay this morning.

2
00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:10.812
Diego Santos: Morning! I’m good, just fighting with my coffee machine again.

3
00:00:11.151 --> 00:00:15.382
Amara Nwosu: Hi all, I’m here. Sorry, my wifi’s a little spotty today.

4
00:00:16.301 --> 00:00:21.685
Omar Haddad: Hey everyone. I’m good, just got back from a long weekend so catching up.

5
00:00:22.239 --> 00:00:28.393
Nadia Petrova: Hi, hi. All good here. I finally finished that Salesforce training, so that’s off my plate.

6
00:00:29.379 --> 00:00:33.995
Rachel Kim: Nice, Nadia! Okay, quick round of updates—anything urgent before we jump in?

7
00:00:35.128 --> 00:00:40.128
Diego Santos: Nothing urgent from me, just the usual Marketo sync delays but nothing new.

8
00:00:40.592 --> 00:00:47.516
Amara Nwosu: I’m still working on the lead routing tweaks, but I’ll ping you, Rachel, if I hit any blockers.

9
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.232
Rachel Kim: Sounds good. Omar, anything from Revenue Analytics?

10
00:00:51.776 --> 00:00:54.852
Omar Haddad: Nope, all quiet. Just prepping for Q2 reporting.

11
00:00:55.688 --> 00:01:01.072
Rachel Kim: Alright, let’s move to the main agenda item: the behavior scoring process in Marketo.

12
00:01:02.449 --> 00:01:11.295
Diego Santos: Yeah, so we’ve had a few questions from the field about when behavior scores reset, and there’s been confusion about the flow limits.

13
00:01:12.595 --> 00:01:19.518
Amara Nwosu: I noticed that too. Some SDRs thought the scores were only applied once per person, not every time.

14
00:01:19.838 --> 00:01:27.531
Rachel Kim: Right, so the current handbook page says ‘every time’ for most behaviors, but it’s not super clear in the table.

15
00:01:28.281 --> 00:01:35.589
Nadia Petrova: I think the confusion is mostly around the ‘Attended Online’ and ‘Attended Webcast’ rows. The descriptions overlap a bit.

16
00:01:36.149 --> 00:01:46.533
Diego Santos: Yeah, and the ‘flow limit’ column wasn’t always filled in, so I updated the draft to say ‘every time’ for all of them, just to be explicit.

17
00:01:46.738 --> 00:01:53.276
Omar Haddad: Wait, so just to clarify, if someone attends three webcasts, they get the points three times, right?

18
00:01:54.479 --> 00:02:00.633
Diego Santos: Exactly. As long as their Person Score is under 300, they get the points each time.

19
00:02:01.661 --> 00:02:07.815
Amara Nwosu: And after 300, the behavior scoring is nulled, so no more points until it drops below?

20
00:02:08.355 --> 00:02:12.201
Diego Santos: Correct. That’s how it’s set up in Marketo right now.

21
00:02:13.365 --> 00:02:19.903
Rachel Kim: Okay, so the proposed change is just making the ‘every time’ language consistent in the table, right?

22
00:02:20.821 --> 00:02:27.359
Diego Santos: Yep, and making sure the flow limit is filled in for all behaviors, so there’s no ambiguity.

23
00:02:28.115 --> 00:02:36.577
Nadia Petrova: I have a small concern—will this affect any of the existing nurture programs? Like, will people suddenly get more points than before?

24
00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:42.558
Diego Santos: No, it’s just a documentation update. The backend logic hasn’t changed, it’s just clearer now.

25
00:02:42.856 --> 00:02:52.472
Omar Haddad: Yeah, I checked the scoring logs last week, and the points are being applied as expected. This just makes it easier for everyone to understand.

26
00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:58.820
Amara Nwosu: I think it’ll help with the SDR questions too. I’m good with it.

27
00:03:00.195 --> 00:03:01.733
Rachel Kim: Alright, any other concerns?

28
00:03:02.642 --> 00:03:04.566
Nadia Petrova: Nope, that answers my question.

29
00:03:05.851 --> 00:03:17.389
Rachel Kim: Great. So, decision: we’ll update the handbook page to clarify that behavior scoring is applied every time for all listed behaviors, as long as the Person Score is under 300.

30
00:03:18.346 --> 00:03:26.808
Diego Santos: I’ll take the action to update the handbook page. Should be able to get that done in the next week or two.

31
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.816
Rachel Kim: Thanks, Diego. Please just drop a note in #marketing-ops when it’s live.

32
00:03:32.181 --> 00:03:33.681
Diego Santos: Will do.

33
00:03:34.282 --> 00:03:45.436
Rachel Kim: Okay, last agenda item—quick one. We got the results from the vendor satisfaction survey. Overall, people are happy, but there were a few comments about slow support from Leadspace.

34
00:03:46.805 --> 00:03:52.574
Omar Haddad: Yeah, I saw that. I think we should flag it for our next renewal discussion.

35
00:03:53.809 --> 00:03:58.424
Nadia Petrova: Agreed. Maybe we can ask for a dedicated support contact or something.

36
00:03:59.743 --> 00:04:05.128
Rachel Kim: Good idea. Diego, can you add that to our vendor review checklist for Q3?

37
00:04:06.496 --> 00:04:10.342
Diego Santos: Yep, I’ll make a note and update the checklist doc.

38
00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:12.316
Amara Nwosu: Thanks, Diego.

39
00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:17.071
Rachel Kim: Alright, that’s it from my side. Anything else before we wrap?

40
00:04:17.404 --> 00:04:18.904
Omar Haddad: Nothing from me.

41
00:04:20.119 --> 00:04:21.619
Nadia Petrova: All good here.

42
00:04:22.968 --> 00:04:24.506
Amara Nwosu: I’m good, thanks everyone.

43
00:04:25.235 --> 00:04:26.735
Diego Santos: All set.

44
00:04:27.742 --> 00:04:31.588
Rachel Kim: Okay, thanks all. Have a great rest of your week!

45
00:04:31.989 --> 00:04:33.489
Omar Haddad: You too!

46
00:04:34.897 --> 00:04:36.397
Nadia Petrova: Bye!

47
00:04:37.721 --> 00:04:39.221
Amara Nwosu: Bye everyone.

48
00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:42.190
Diego Santos: Bye!

49
00:04:43.444 --> 00:04:44.944
Rachel Kim: Meeting adjourned, thanks!
